  "query_preview": "..\n\nBrief period of increasing subsidence will mark this early morning \nperiod as high based scattered shower activity from decaying MCS \ncontinues to decrease in coverage. Attention quickly turns to an \nexpanding area of precipitation this morning that will continue \nthrough today. Widespread/numerous showers and scattered \nthunderstorms will be forced along a warm frontal zone and theta-e \nridge with additional support from the low level jet, though \nstrongest LLJ winds hold to the west. The warm front sets up over \nLower Michigan this morning then lifts northward towards the I-69 \ncorridor later today focusing the higher coverage of showers with \ntime across northern Lower Michigan and southeast through the Thumb. \nThe overall airmass will be quite moist as dewpoints climb into the \n60s and PWATS values increase to 1.75 inches or greater across \nsoutheast Michigan while approaching 2 inches over the Thumb. \nOverall severe threat will be very limited with the lack of surface \ninstability and mean MUCAPE values generally around 500 J/kg or \nless, peaking later this afternoon and evening. Main concern will be \nfor repeated rounds of showers and thunderstorms that could bring \nlocally heavy rainfall. After a brief period of lower QPF potential \nlater this evening, an additional uptick in convective potential \noccurs late this evening through Monday morning as a cold front then \ndrops into northern lower Michigan support a corridor of more focused\nforcing. While shear will remain favorable, severe potential again \nlooks limited given the weaker instability. Locally heavy rainfall \nwill continue to be the main concern.\n\nModel consensus puts the axis of heavier precipitation focused along \nand north of a line from Midland to Port Huron. Average total \nrainfall amounts to around an inch are forecast in this axis \nstarting this morning (12Z) to early Monday afternoon (18Z). \nA localized swath of 2 to near 4 inches of total rainfall will be \npossible across parts of the Thumb, which is highlighted well in the \nHREF 24-hr QPF ensemble localized probability-matched mean. Amounts \nto half an inch are forecast down to roughly the M-59 corridor and a \nquarter inch or less south of M-59 down to the southern border. \n\nSoutheast Michigan will reside on the northern end of a ridge during \nthe early to mid-week period. Slight increase in heat and humidity \nwill arrive with the persistent west-southwest low to mid level \nflow. Embedded shortwaves within this flow around the ridge will \ncontinue to offer chances of precipitation into mid-week. Given the \npattern and potential for continued chances of rainfall and \nincreased cloud cover, current NBM forecast temperatures are \nprobably on the high side mid-week if that pans out. Michigan will \nremain in a corridor of higher PWATs, which keep the potential for \nlocalized heavy rainfall as a concern during this stretch and daily \norganized convective chances will depend on the degree of \ninstability that is able to develop each day.",
